---
description: Apply when writing or reviewing code that handles untrusted input, auth, secrets, payments, or file/shell/database access — and when asked for a security audit
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- A vulnerability is a path from attacker-controlled input to a sensitive operation. Data from users — including data users wrote that comes back out of the database — is untrusted on every path.
- Never concatenate untrusted data into an interpreter's input (SQL, shell, paths, templates, HTML). Use the standard mechanism: parameterized queries, argument arrays, path allowlists, framework escaping — never a custom sanitizer.
- Authorize on every path, not just the front door: object IDs checked against the caller's permissions (IDOR), background/admin/API routes enforcing the same checks as the UI route, JWT signature/expiry actually validated.
- Secrets stay out of code, logs, errors, and URLs; committed-to-git means exposed — rotate, don't delete. Verify TLS; CSPRNG for tokens; no homemade crypto. No deserialization/eval of untrusted data (pickle, `yaml.load`).
- Auditing: a finding requires a concrete exploit scenario ("attacker sends X → flows through Y unsanitized (file:line) → achieves Z"), labeled CONFIRMED (traced) or PLAUSIBLE (+ what would confirm). No scenario → not a finding.
- Audit report: `SEVERITY [CONFIRMED|PLAUSIBLE] path:line — vulnerability — exploit scenario — remediation`. Skip the noise categories unless asked: DoS, rate limiting, missing hardening, theoretical races, outdated-dep CVEs (one "update X" line), ReDoS, test-only files.
- Don't fix during an audit — report first; fixes are a separate reviewed change.
